(5514339, 792487, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Jutilda:</b></div><hr/><i> I don''t see how anyone could even come close to saying DNMC.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>I''m sure it was the specificity of the challenge description:<br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Challenge Description:</b></div><hr/><i>:	Depict quitting this week in your entry... <b>as long it''s not quitting DPC.</b> :)</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Without the title it could refer to photography in general, but the reference to low scores seems to tie it directly to the DPC experience.<br/> <br/>I didn''t vote on this one, and that''s not necessarily my own opinion of the shot, but it''s how I''d answer your question.</td>', 'Originally posted by Jutilda: I don''t see how anyone could even come close to saying DNMC. I''m sure it was the specificity of the challenge description:Originally posted by Challenge Description::	Depict quitting this week in your entry... as long it''s not quitting DPC. :) Without the title it could refer to photography in general, but the reference to low scores seems to tie it directly to the DPC experience. I didn''t vote on this one, and that''s not necessarily my own opinion of the shot, but it''s how I''d answer your question.', '2009-06-04 16:10:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(5514635, 792877, 31637, '<td valign="top">This is a very powerful image indeed, I applaud you for being brave for this one</td>', 'This is a very powerful image indeed, I applaud you for being brave for this one', '2009-06-04 20:12:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(5514794, 792487, 97166, '<td valign="top">I would have voted it lower for partly-DNMC.<br/> <br/>I''ll be honest about that.  Good thing I didn''t vote I guess, but I would have given it a reactionary 3.<br/> <br/>Why?<br/> <br/>Because while yes, you can quit photography without quitting DPC, that''s a bloody cop-out answer to me.  It''s like being told you can''t use wine glasses in a still life, but then using a beer glass instead.  It''s staying too close to a comfort level because nothing else is hitting you creatively.<br/> <br/>But hey, it worked, it ribboned, life moves on right? :)<br/> <br/>I just wanted to throw in my support for the DNMC''ers.  I''m with you people.</td>', 'I would have voted it lower for partly-DNMC. I''ll be honest about that.  Good thing I didn''t vote I guess, but I would have given it a reactionary 3. Why? Because while yes, you can quit photography without quitting DPC, that''s a bloody cop-out answer to me.  It''s like being told you can''t use wine glasses in a still life, but then using a beer glass instead.  It''s staying too close to a comfort level because nothing else is hitting you creatively. But hey, it worked, it ribboned, life moves on right? :) I just wanted to throw in my support for the DNMC''ers.  I''m with you people.', '2009-06-04 21:54:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5514861, 793905, 38930, '<td valign="top">This is toned really well.  The picture is great, but the toning adds so much more!!</td>', 'This is toned really well.  The picture is great, but the toning adds so much more!!', '2009-06-04 22:46:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(5514892, 793729, 91282, '<td valign="top">I disagree with the people who say the keyboard should be cleaner; You''re a gamer, which means your keyboard gets used a lot. Its going to be dirty. :P Honestly, I think some of the letters should be partially rubbed off, too. Lord knows my WASD keys are practically illegible with all the gaming I do. lol</td>', 'I disagree with the people who say the keyboard should be cleaner; You''re a gamer, which means your keyboard gets used a lot. Its going to be dirty. :P Honestly, I think some of the letters should be partially rubbed off, too. Lord knows my WASD keys are practically illegible with all the gaming I do. lol', '2009-06-04 23:03:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5515002, 792487, 54645, '<td valign="top">Life is rough at the top!</td>', 'Life is rough at the top!', '2009-06-05 00:22:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5515141, 792487, 30982, '<td valign="top">See, now this backdrop behind your glamour shot may have worked very well!  (Congrats on the ribbon, too, by the way.)</td>', 'See, now this backdrop behind your glamour shot may have worked very well!  (Congrats on the ribbon, too, by the way.)', '2009-06-05 03:00:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5515586, 792487, 70128, '<td valign="top">Lydia, please! If I were to quit for my low scores, I would have been gone even before my first post!! LOL<br/>And OUCH! Poor camera! If you don''t want it anymore, I could make good use of it!!! LOL <br/>Anyway, my true congrats to you, for your efforts and perseverance, but mainly for the great idea and shot!</td>', 'Lydia, please! If I were to quit for my low scores, I would have been gone even before my first post!! LOLAnd OUCH! Poor camera! If you don''t want it anymore, I could make good use of it!!! LOL Anyway, my true congrats to you, for your efforts and perseverance, but mainly for the great idea and shot!', '2009-06-05 13:10:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(5515946, 792487, 54645, '<td valign="top">Lydia, less than 4 percent of the voters likely agreed with <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=97166" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">k10dguy</a>. Like I said before, life is rough at the top! There are always people looking to degrade accomplishments of others rather than be gracious. <br/> <br/>Deb made a good point, this is a good background for a portrait.</td>', 'Lydia, less than 4 percent of the voters likely agreed with  k10dguy. Like I said before, life is rough at the top! There are always people looking to degrade accomplishments of others rather than be gracious.  Deb made a good point, this is a good background for a portrait.', '2009-06-05 18:39:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5516892, 793905, 16266, '<td valign="top">haha.. what a great image! :)</td>', 'haha.. what a great image! :)', '2009-06-06 13:44:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5518123, 793876, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>This is starting to get weird now! I''m wondering if anybody else is ever going to crop in my queue. I think this is my 6 critique for you in the last few weeks.<br/> <br/>I wrote a big long critique and then my machine got stuck and I needed to reboot, so just lost it all.<br/> <br/>I am going to start this critique by asking you, a fellow critique club member, how you would critique this image?<br/> <br/>I have a couple of questions about it.  When you entered this into the I Quit challenge, did you really think it actually met the challenge, or did you think that you could squeeze it in with the title? (I''m assuming you did think it met the challenge or your would not have entered it) Do you yourself like it? What score would you give it in light of the challenge topic?  <br/> <br/>I ask the questions, just as a pre-amble to my critique, because my words may sound harsh, but I think if you put this up for critique, then you want real feedback and not just platitudes. <br/> <br/>Technically this image is lacking. The exposure is off, it lacks contrast and the colours are washed out.  Some of these things could have been fixed in camera by using the correct exposure, but a lot could be done in post processing since the highlights and shadows are not clipped.  I''d be very interested to see the original file, as you have listed a whole bunch of editing steps, but I am not sure what those have done to this one.  It may have been a good idea to boost the ISO to 400, it may have given you a faster shutter speed which in turn may have made the image a bit sharper.  Even with VR 1/30 is quite a slow shutter speed and could easily give you a bit of camera shake.  Typically anything lower than 1/60 is going to cause some kind of camera shake.  <br/> <br/>Coming back to the concept. I''m afraid to say I see this as a real shoe horn for this challenge.  The image itself does not really portray quitting, it relies on the title to link it to the challenge topic. You were far from the only person to do that. I think this was a really difficult challenge, so kudos for giving it a go. <br/> <br/>Susan, I''m sorry if this critique sounds harsh, it''s not meant to be and I certainly do not want to discourage you.  Having critiqued so many of your images just recently, I would just like to help you a little bit.  <br/> <br/>Sarah<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club This is starting to get weird now! I''m wondering if anybody else is ever going to crop in my queue. I think this is my 6 critique for you in the last few weeks. I wrote a big long critique and then my machine got stuck and I needed to reboot, so just lost it all. I am going to start this critique by asking you, a fellow critique club member, how you would critique this image? I have a couple of questions about it.  When you entered this into the I Quit challenge, did you really think it actually met the challenge, or did you think that you could squeeze it in with the title? (I''m assuming you did think it met the challenge or your would not have entered it) Do you yourself like it? What score would you give it in light of the challenge topic?   I ask the questions, just as a pre-amble to my critique, because my words may sound harsh, but I think if you put this up for critique, then you want real feedback and not just platitudes.  Technically this image is lacking. The exposure is off, it lacks contrast and the colours are washed out.  Some of these things could have been fixed in camera by using the correct exposure, but a lot could be done in post processing since the highlights and shadows are not clipped.  I''d be very interested to see the original file, as you have listed a whole bunch of editing steps, but I am not sure what those have done to this one.  It may have been a good idea to boost the ISO to 400, it may have given you a faster shutter speed which in turn may have made the image a bit sharper.  Even with VR 1/30 is quite a slow shutter speed and could easily give you a bit of camera shake.  Typically anything lower than 1/60 is going to cause some kind of camera shake.   Coming back to the concept. I''m afraid to say I see this as a real shoe horn for this challenge.  The image itself does not really portray quitting, it relies on the title to link it to the challenge topic. You were far from the only person to do that. I think this was a really difficult challenge, so kudos for giving it a go.  Susan, I''m sorry if this critique sounds harsh, it''s not meant to be and I certainly do not want to discourage you.  Having critiqued so many of your images just recently, I would just like to help you a little bit.   Sarah', '2009-06-07 11:18:10.000000', NULL, 0),
